.title  The game on the 386: double words and a clock of its own
.what   Lesson 23's flight rewritten for the 80386 in one file, CLOCK.ASM, with TICK.ASM before it. TICK is the timer chip on its own: the crystal's 1193182 pulses, a divider of 16384 instead of 65536, INT 8 taken, 72.8 ticks a second, every fourth handed to the BIOS, and all of it given back at the end. CLOCK asks NEED386 first, fills four pixels a write with REP STOSD and REP MOVSD, draws every polygon with one divide an edge and then only adds, x held as 16.16 in a double word, and draws the picture between the steps of the physics, which stays on 18.2. The picture's angles count in eighths of a step, 2048 to the circle, so a bank turns without jumps. No slow mode: the F key and the meter are gone
.tier   LESSON
.main   CLOCK.ASM
.needs  TICK.ASM
.build  FASM TICK.ASM TICK.COM
.build  FASM CLOCK.ASM CLOCK.COM
.run    CLOCK.COM
.uses   STOSD MOVSD OUT IRET MOVSX MOVZX IDIV SAR
.engine ENGINE\E_CPU.INC ENGINE\E_MATH.INC ENGINE\E_TERR.INC ENGINE\E_M3D.INC ENGINE\E_RAST.INC
